There is no mathematics on this page. It holds no formulas, derivations or computations. Folio 57r is a page of Riemann's reading excerpts headed '[?] aus Herbart's Werken'. It continues the Herbart/Locke/Leibniz excerpts of the previous page and leads into the excerpts on 'Raum (Materie)' and Herbart's 'De attractione elementorum' on the next page.

Contents in order:

(1) Psychologie als Wissenschaft, Bd. 2, Vorrede p. XXIV (Werke VI, 16). Herbart says the general concepts of the calculus matter more than the computations themselves for avoiding gross errors in psychology. A full working-out of mathematical psychology would need a highly trained mathematical mind. That mind would construct the possible cases from the speculative principles and pick out those that fit given psychological phenomena. Herbart disclaims being a mathematician himself.

(2) Allgemeine Metaphysik II, 190 (W. III, 201). History of philosophy is the dullest of histories unless it is used for new philosophising. One can borrow proofs from it, but not aids of exposition. Presenting metaphysics so that it is neither misunderstood nor left unintelligible is very hard.

(3) Psychologie Bd. 2, p. 532 (W. VI, 455). Whether psychology can match the natural sciences in exact, mathematical explanation of facts cannot yet be said. Its observations lack precision. Direct comparison between the speculative theory and observation will rarely be possible, so researchers must work 'as if in the dark'. It requires a particular turn of mind and training. 'No one will grasp psychology firmly whose general metaphysics is still wavering' (underlined by Riemann).

(4) A biographical chronology of J. F. Herbart:
- born 4 May 1776 in Oldenburg; father a Justizrath;
- earliest essay, on human freedom, 1790 (Gymnasium Oldenburg);
- Jena from Easter 1794;
- tutor to the Steiger von Riggisberg family (Landvogt of Interlaken) from March 1797;
- 1800 via Jena to Göttingen and Oldenburg, then Bremen (with Johann Smidt);
- Göttingen from spring 1802;
- first psychology lectures 1806–07 and 1808–09;
- Königsberg from Easter 1809;
- married Miss Drake, an Englishwoman raised in Königsberg, 13 June 1811;
- hoped in vain for a Berlin call after Hegel's death;
- call to Göttingen early 1833, leaving Königsberg after 7 September;
- died of a stroke on 14 August 1841 at 3 a.m.

(5) A quotation from the preface dated 14 Dec. 1812 of the Lehrbuch zur Einleitung in die Philosophie. Herbart was urged to print a textbook (Leitfaden) for his lectures.

(6) Herbart Werke Bd. 3, p. 353, on practical philosophy and religion. The 'Sollen' (ethics) and religious belief both spring from one root, the aesthetic judgement. They are then supported and matured by social need, by the feeling of dependence, and by teleological contemplation of nature.

Of mathematical interest is only Riemann's evident attention to Herbart's call for a mathematician to develop mathematical psychology. This fits Riemann's known self-description as a Herbartian in psychology and epistemology.
